ing to scale  they can just assume that it exists and  kubernetes will make sure that it's  available wherever their workload is  scheduled and move around with it  wherever their workload is scheduled and  then what about the cross-section of the  hard problems around storage and network  right much of the storages  network-attached right so you've clearly  got to have a tight correlation with the  storage the storage saying in the  networks they have to be pretty tightly  correlated right yes a lot of the magic  that comes from being able to move your  workloads around with your storage is  due to the fact that the story just  actually network-attached that does come  in a cost if you you're networking your  storage is going over your network your  network and your network constrained it  can it can cause issues here  unfortunately in kubernetes we don't do  a great job of being able to partition  out your network i/o so sad  you've mentioned six storage a couple of  times now and it sounds like you're in  the leadership position around the  things and have been around that maybe  tell us a little bit about how the  special interest groups work in the in  the CN CF and around kubernetes and  maybe some best practices you've learned  and sure in working with that yeah so  the special interest groups and  kubernetes are designed to kind of break  this very very large project into more  manageable chunks trying to manage a  project of the size of kubernetes an  open source project where you don't have  hierarchy you don't have you know folks  who are in charge of large groups is  very difficult and so with the special  interest groups what we've tried to do  is create areas of interest where you  can have developers focused on a  specific area of kubernetes so for  example I work with cig storage and  we're responsible for the volume  subsystem of kubernetes and if someone  wanted to get involved in your sake or  another say what's that process looked  at so we hold 